hypotheses.statement:
The solutions satisfy $0\le f\le tM$. On every fixed compact subset, including $S$, $$\label{eq:ah-s-floor}
 s\ge c_{\mathrm{loc}}(M,L)\epsilon ,$$ and their height gradients have finite bounds for fixed $M,L,\epsilon$, independent of $d_*$, $R$, and derivatives of $b_S$. Moreover, $$\begin{aligned}
 \int_{\{f\in I\}}p\cdot df&\le C|I|,\qquad
 \int_{\Omega_R}(W^2-1)\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dV_g\le CM,\label{eq:ah-height-energy}\\
 |f|&\le C R_s^{-1}(R_s/r)^{4-\xi},\nonumber\\
 |v|+|\pi|+|\nabla\pi|&\le C(R_s/r)^{3-\xi}
       \quad(r\ge C_0R_s),\label{eq:ah-end-graph}
\end{aligned}$$ where $I$ is any height interval, $\xi>0$ can be fixed sufficiently small, and the constants in <a href="#eq:ah-height-energy" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:ah-height-energy">[eq:ah-height-energy]</a>–<a href="#eq:ah-end-graph" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:ah-end-graph">[eq:ah-end-graph]</a> are independent of $M,L,\epsilon,d_*$ for large $M$. The end estimates include the corresponding local Hölder control and derivatives of $v$ through order two.

outputs.exact_statement:
Under <a href="#eq:decay" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:decay">[eq:decay]</a>–<a href="#eq:future-trapping" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:future-trapping">[eq:future-trapping]</a>, the completeness, one-end, and smoothness assumptions above, and the future-timelike condition <a href="#eq:invariant-mass" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:invariant-mass">[eq:invariant-mass]</a>, one has $$\label{eq:main-inequality}
 m_{\ensuremath{\mathrm{AH}}}(g)\ge\frac12\bigl(r_A+r_A^3\bigr),
 \qquad r_A=\sqrt{\frac{A_{\min}(S;g)}{4\pi}}.$$ The coefficient is sharp: equality holds for every positive-mass time-symmetric Schwarzschild–anti-de Sitter exterior bounded by its minimal horizon.

Suppose the adjusted metric satisfies $\mathop{\mathrm{Scal}}_{\widetilde h}\ge-C_0$ outside $E_0$, and let $A_*>0$. At almost every time for which $A(t)\le A_*$, $$\label{eq:flow-uniform-curvature}
 J(t):=\int_{N_t}|\nabla u|^2\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dA_{\widetilde h}
 \le C_H:=1+\frac23 C_0 A_*+16\pi b_2.$$ In particular this bound is independent of $A(0)$, even if the initial areas tend to zero in a family.

Let $N$ be a compact embedded $C^1$ enclosure of $O$, disjoint from $S$, with connected exterior.*


1.  *On the original $\ensuremath{\mathrm{AH}}$ exterior, fix a large radius $r_p$ and put $$\label{eq:flow-ah-weight}
     \rho=\min\{1,r_p^2/r^2\},$$ with $\rho=1$ on the compact part. There is $q(r_p)\downarrow0$, depending only on the original end metric, such that $$\label{eq:flow-ah-projection}
     \int_N\rho\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dA_g\ge(1-q(r_p))A_*.$$*

2.  *Suppose the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{AF}}$ extension has the parallel-surface form $g_o=U^2\mathop{}\!\mathrm dt^2+\gamma_t$, where $\gamma_t$ is induced on outward Euclidean parallels of a strictly convex embedding of $(\mathcal C,\gamma_0)$. Then, with $\rho=1$, $$\label{eq:flow-af-projection}
     \int_N\mathop{}\!\mathrm dA_{g_{\mathrm{base}}}\ge A_*.$$ For a surface crossing the joining sphere, the assertion holds for the piecewise area; it is enough that its intersection with the joining sphere have zero two-dimensional measure. In particular this holds for almost every weak-flow level.

Fix the capped topology $(X,O)$, a number $A_*>0$, and a scalar lower bound $C_R\ge0$. Suppose a comparison has the following properties.*

1.  *The smooth complete metric $\widehat h$ has one $\ensuremath{\mathrm{AH}}$ or $\ensuremath{\mathrm{AF}}$ end with the mean-curvature and radial-gradient asymptotics used in <a href="#eq:flow-end-subsolution" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:flow-end-subsolution">[eq:flow-end-subsolution]</a>. The cap-only enclosure $E_0$ is a smooth precompact strictly outward-minimizing set, with minimal boundary disjoint from $O$, every component meeting $O$, and $\mathop{\mathrm{Scal}}_{\widehat h}\ge-C_R$ outside $E_0$.*


2.  *On $X\setminus O$ there are a reference metric $g_{\mathrm{base}}$, positive functions $x,W$, a metric $\bar g$, and a weight $0<\rho\le1$, such that $$\label{eq:flow-metric-comparison}
     \bar g\ge g_{\mathrm{base}},\qquad
     \mathop{}\!\mathrm dV_{\bar g}=W\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dV_{g_{\mathrm{base}}},\qquad
     (1-\eta)x\bar g\le\widehat h\le(1+\eta)x\bar g.$$*


3.  *Every enclosing $C^1$ surface with connected exterior, as in Lemma <a href="#lem:area-projection" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:area-projection">18</a>, satisfies $$\label{eq:flow-abstract-projection}
     \int_N\rho\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dA_{g_{\mathrm{base}}}\ge(1-q)A_*.$$ In the piecewise case this is required for surfaces having zero area in the joining surface.*


*For every $0<\varepsilon<1$ there are positive tolerances $\eta_0,q_0,\delta_0$ and $v_0$, depending only on $\varepsilon,A_*,C_R$ and the integer $b_2$ in <a href="#eq:flow-topology-constant" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:flow-topology-constant">[eq:flow-topology-constant]</a>, with the following consequence. If $\eta\le\eta_0$, $q\le q_0$, $0<\delta\le\delta_0$, and $$\label{eq:flow-bad-volume}
 \int_{\{x<1-\delta\}}\rho^{3/2}W\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dV_{g_{\mathrm{base}}}<v_0,$$ then $$\label{eq:flow-area-conclusion}
 |\partial E_0|_{\widehat h}\ge(1-\varepsilon)A_*.$$ The tolerances are uniform over all comparisons satisfying these conditions; the geometry and initial area of $E_0$ may vary.

The solutions satisfy $0\le f\le tM$. On every fixed compact subset, including $S$, $$\label{eq:ah-s-floor}
 s\ge c_{\mathrm{loc}}(M,L)\epsilon ,$$ and their height gradients have finite bounds for fixed $M,L,\epsilon$, independent of $d_*$, $R$, and derivatives of $b_S$. Moreover, $$\begin{aligned}
 \int_{\{f\in I\}}p\cdot df&\le C|I|,\qquad
 \int_{\Omega_R}(W^2-1)\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dV_g\le CM,\label{eq:ah-height-energy}\\
 |f|&\le C R_s^{-1}(R_s/r)^{4-\xi},\nonumber\\
 |v|+|\pi|+|\nabla\pi|&\le C(R_s/r)^{3-\xi}
       \quad(r\ge C_0R_s),\label{eq:ah-end-graph}
\end{aligned}$$ where $I$ is any height interval, $\xi>0$ can be fixed sufficiently small, and the constants in <a href="#eq:ah-height-energy" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:ah-height-energy">[eq:ah-height-energy]</a>–<a href="#eq:ah-end-graph" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:ah-end-graph">[eq:ah-end-graph]</a> are independent of $M,L,\epsilon,d_*$ for large $M$. The end estimates include the corresponding local Hölder control and derivatives of $v$ through order two.

With internal parameters fixed, the fields $N,f,df,w$ are uniformly precompact in local uniform topology, including at $S$. Limit solutions have $N,w>0$ and are classical away from $S$. They obey $$\label{eq:ah-conformal-decay}
 x-1=O_{\mathrm{fixed},2,\gamma}(r^{-3+\xi}).$$ Here $\xi$ is taken sufficiently small, in particular $0<\xi<\min(3-\tau,\tau-1)$. Their metrics $h$ satisfy the required asymptotically hyperbolic decay, all four mass fluxes are finite, and $V_0(\mathop{\mathrm{Scal}}_h+6)$ is integrable near infinity. Their time components satisfy $$\label{eq:ah-mass-budget}
 8\pi\{p_0(h)-p_0(g)\}
   =t\int_S b_S\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dA_g
      +\int_\Omega\{(x-1)\Sigma+P\}\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm dV_g
   \le o(1)\quad(M\to\infty).$$ The $o(1)$ is uniform over the allowed data, with the subsequent choices of $\epsilon,d_*$ just described.

Fix base data satisfying <a href="#eq:compact-base-data" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:compact-base-data">[eq:compact-base-data]</a>. Fix $b_*,\delta_0,\delta,a_o>0$ and $L\ge1$. For every sufficiently large finite $M$, $C_2$ can be chosen depending on these parameters, and then $\varepsilon>0$ can be chosen arbitrarily small and $D_1,D_2$ sufficiently large but finite, so that <a href="#eq:compact-system" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:compact-system">[eq:compact-system]</a>–<a href="#eq:compact-boundary" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:compact-boundary">[eq:compact-boundary]</a> at $u_*=1$ has a positive solution on the full attached exterior. It is smooth on each closed side of $\mathcal C$ and tends to the specified values at infinity. It satisfies $$\label{eq:compact-bounds}
 \begin{gathered}
 0\le f\le M,\qquad N_i,N_o\le C(L)M,\qquad
 \int_{\Omega_i}W^2\le C(L)M^2,\\
 N_i\ge c\varepsilon,\qquad s_i,N_o\ge c(M,L)\varepsilon.
 \end{gathered}$$ The constants in these bounds do not depend on $\varepsilon,D_1,D_2$ or the outer truncation. Dependence on the fixed data and on the preceding parameters is suppressed. At fixed $M,L,\varepsilon$, the lapse and height have uniform local continuity bounds off the seam, including at $S$, independent of $D_1,D_2$. Moreover $$\label{eq:compact-penalty-targets}
 w\ge M^{9/10}\quad\text{where }f\ge9M/10,
 \qquad x\ge1\quad\text{on }\mathcal D_M\cap\{N\le2\varepsilon\},$$ and $$\label{eq:compact-penalty-cost}
 \int P_1\le C(L)M^{-1/10},\qquad
 \int P_2\le\omega_M(\varepsilon),\qquad \omega_M(\varepsilon)\longrightarrow0
 \quad(\varepsilon\downarrow0).$$ The function $\omega_M$ is uniform in the finite strengths and outer radius. In particular $$\label{eq:compact-bad-volume}
 \int_{\mathcal D_M\cap\{x<1-\delta\}}W\le C/L,$$ where $C$ is independent of $L,M,\varepsilon,D_1,D_2$. On the smooth sides the metric $h=x\bar g$ obeys the scalar and boundary identities <a href="#eq:compact-scalar" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:compact-scalar">[eq:compact-scalar]</a> and <a href="#eq:compact-mean-curvature" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:compact-mean-curvature">[eq:compact-mean-curvature]</a> below. Its asymptotically flat mass satisfies $$\label{eq:compact-mass-budget}
 m_h\le m_o+C(b_*+\delta_0)+C(L)M^{-1/10}+C\omega_M(\varepsilon).$$ Thus the parameter order is: fix the base data; choose $b_*,\delta_0$ for the mass tolerance; choose $\delta,a_o$ and then $L$ for the area tolerance; choose $M$ large; choose $C_2$; choose $\varepsilon$ small; choose the finite strengths; and finally let the outer radius tend to infinity.

For the state range <a href="#eq:transmission-state-range" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:transmission-state-range">[eq:transmission-state-range]</a>, there are $\gamma>0$ and $C<\infty$ such that $$\|N_i\|_{C^\gamma(\overline\Omega_i)}
 +\|\mathop{}\!\mathrm df\|_{C^\gamma(\overline\Omega_i)}
 +\|N_o\|_{C^\gamma(K\cap\overline\Omega_o)}\leq C
 \label{eq:transmission-holder}$$ on every fixed compact set $K$. The constants are uniform over homotopy zeros and sufficiently large outer truncations. They depend on the finite state range, bounds for the undifferentiated height and lapse sources, and the fixed geometry, but not on derivatives of $T$ or the strength of $P$.
